  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The most iconic lake in the Verdon, formed in 1974 by an EDF dam, with spectacular turquoise waters where the gorges open out. It's one of the most beautiful bodies of water in France, and several of its beaches accept dogs on a leash.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Several beaches line the lake (Sainte-Croix-du-Verdon, Bauduen, Les Salles-sur-Verdon), and each town sets its own rules. At <strong>Les Salles-sur-Verdon</strong>, the Lacs et Gorges du Verdon intercommunal tourist office states that dogs are allowed on a leash on all beaches (Commandon, Charoup, "Au droit des deux campings"…) <strong>except Margaridon beach</strong>, below the village, where the supervised swimming area is in July-August. At <strong>Sainte-Croix-du-Verdon</strong>, access to the beaches towards the dam is banned to everyone. At <strong>Bauduen</strong>, the beach is regulated by municipal order: ask at the tourist office (04 94 50 99 05) or look at the signs before settling in with your dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Pont du Galetas, at the entrance to the gorges, offers a spectacular view — and a good starting point for a canoe trip with your dog.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Verdon gorges themselves are worth the detour, but river swimming there is riskier (cold water, currents) — it's better to save swimming for the lake itself.</p>
    </div>

